## Feature #7 Increment 3 (spec §4.7) — Codex MEDIUM guard: nested/repeated
## `TestClock.install` must NOT re-anchor the active virtual clock, and an INNER
## `uninstall` must NOT restore the real clock while an OUTER install is still
## live. install/uninstall are NESTING-AWARE (depth-counted): only the outermost
## install anchors (now=0, mode=1) and only the outermost uninstall restores.
##
## Scenario:
##   1. OUTER install → virtual, now=0. advance 30s → now=30s.
##   2. INNER install (nested) → must NOT reset now to 0 (still 30s). Register a
##      timer at +10s → deadline 40s.
##   3. INNER uninstall → depth still 1 (outer live) → clock STAYS virtual.
##   4. advance 15s (now=45s ≥ 40s deadline) → the timer fires. If the inner
##      install had reset now to 0, or the inner uninstall had gone real, this
##      would NOT fire under virtual time. Firing proves nesting correctness.
##   5. OUTER uninstall → depth 0 → real clock restored.

use std.time.Duration

def main
  puts "nest start"
  TestClock.install                    # outer (depth 1): now=0
  var cx = Context.executor
  let h = ruxen_reactor_current_handle()

  TestClock.advance(Duration.from_secs(30))   # now=30s

  TestClock.install                    # inner (depth 2): must NOT reset now
  let t = ruxen_reactor_register_timer(h, 10000000000)  # deadline 30+10 = 40s
  puts "fired_at_reg=#{ruxen_reactor_check_fired(h, t)}"  # 0 (not yet due)

  TestClock.uninstall                  # inner drop (depth 1): clock STAYS virtual

  TestClock.advance(Duration.from_secs(15))   # now=45s ≥ 40s → fires
  puts "fired_after=#{ruxen_reactor_check_fired(h, t)}"   # 1

  TestClock.uninstall                  # outer (depth 0): real clock restored
  puts "nest done"
end
